WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.260
What can you tell me about the street names of Mossville?

2
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.760
Prater Road is named after my grandfather [Earthy Prater].

3
00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:14.680
It was nothing but woods from Old Spanish Trail to Highway 90.

4
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:22.120
And once he moved from the corner of Evergreen and Old Spanish Trail, which was a large,

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.100
large house that had a hall that ran from the front door to the back door.

6
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.220
The Praters lived on one side and the Lemelles lived on the other side.

7
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:32.980
So my grandfather, Earthy Prater,

8
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:42.180
took a horse and a wagon and kind of carved out a trail that we now call Prater Road and he built their home on that.

9
00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.500
And the home was still standing until [Hurricanes] Rita and Katrina came through.

10
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.900
And it was demolished at that particular time.

11
00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:55.620
These particular areas in here, Moss Avenue is named after, I believe, the Edy Mosses,

12
00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:59.740
because that’s who lived across the street where the big, big oak trees are.

13
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:10.520
And then some of the others was just named after local people or some of the merchants that had came in within the last forty or fifty years.

14
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.507
. . . So if you lived here long enough, then the road just got named after you eventually?

15
00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:16.440
[Agrees] Right.

16
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.600
That’s easier.

17
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.880
More or less.

18
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.980
I think they named Coach Williams [Drive] about maybe three, four years ago.

19
00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:31.020
And I think they named it because he had been in this area for a number of years and had worked as a teacher,

20
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.440
a principal, and then on the sheriff department force and everything.

21
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.224
So they gave it to him as an honorary name.

22
00:01:36.224 --> 00:01:36.620
Got you.

23
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:46.560
And Benjamin, possibly, I can’t swear to it, on the next corner over there, was a man that was blind.

24
00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.280
And his first name was Benny [Benjamin Harris].

25
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:51.840
So more than likely it was named after him.

26
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.440
So most of the street names are family . . .

27
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:53.760
Family names.

28
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:53.960
Yes.

29
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.840
. . . last names.

30
00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.780
What about the King and the Prince and the Duchess roads?

31
00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.380
Was that Mossville?

32
00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:08.460
That was a subdivision that was probably added in the ‘50s.

33
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.700
Because when I went . . . I graduated in 1962.

34
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:21.740
And in that area, there was maybe at that time ten, twelve homes that was back there.

35
00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:31.200
So someone had the property, divided it into subdivisions, and it was that person who decided to name it.

36
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.280
I’m thinking it was someone who was white who had purchased the property.

37
00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:39.880
And then they made the subdivisions and named it what they wanted to name it.

38
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:46.760
Because across the street over there [Perkins Avenue], unless it’s changed recently, Powell Lumber owned all of that over there.

39
00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.800
So it’s possible that they could have sold a tract of land, whatever, added that.
